WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.968
-Thank you for doing
this Christmas movie different.

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:04.767
I appreciate you doing that.
We hung out all day together.

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:06.067
-How fun with that?
-It was the greatest.

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.367
We always -- We --
-Two-take maximum.

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:09.701
[ Laughter ]
Two-take maximum.

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:11.534
-We nailed it!
We had some good takes.

00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:13.801
It was so much fun.
Happy holidays to you and --

00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:15.067
and your family.
-You too.

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:16.267
-I'm happy to see you in person.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:18.100
Thanks for making the trip
and being here.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.667
-Absolutely.
-Well, what are your --

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:21.300
Do you have, like,
family traditions?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.734
Do you have holiday plans?
What do you have coming up?

00:00:23.734 --> 00:00:25.567
-This year, we're gonna get
all the McConaughey family.

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:27.934
We're also getting
Camila's family.

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:30.000
So when we do that --
When we have that

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
multicultural Christmas,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.834
Christmas lasts a long time
at our place.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.167
Because they celebrate
on the 24th.

00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:39.367
Midnight, you exchange presents,
all right?

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:41.734
So you have
the big sit-down dinner.

00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:43.968
You get the china out
for the first time all year.

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:45.300
[ Laughter ]
If it was up to my family,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.167
it'd be paper plates.
Thank you, Camila.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:48.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:50.501
-Get the china out.
-A better ritual.

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:51.801
-"No, we are using china."
-Yes.

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:53.868
-Thank you, Camila, yeah.
-And then we do that that night,

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:56.734
and then we get up the next day,
and we do our Christmas,

00:00:56.734 --> 00:00:59.267
which is everyone
opens presents, Santa came.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.534
The problem, the reason I say
it's longer already,

00:01:01.534 --> 00:01:04.968
is that my mother, who's 90,
still demands that you --

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:07.901
everyone open their present,
and everyone watch one person

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.067
open their present at a time.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:10.767
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-If you got a big

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.834
Brazilian family, you got a big
McConaughey family.

00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:14.067
There's a family of 20.

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.701
There's a lot of presents,
thankfully.

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.901
-Yes.
-It may be dark on the 25th

00:01:17.901 --> 00:01:20.567
when we're done opening presents
from that morning.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.634
And so, it's 7:00 p.m.,
and you just finished

00:01:22.634 --> 00:01:24.367
opening presents
from Christmas morning.

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:26.467
-Yeah.
-So the nights get long.

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.667
-My grandma used to do this
thing where we'd unwrap

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:30.400
the presents, she goes,
"Can you just not rip it

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.834
just so I could reuse
the wrapping?"

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:33.300
[ Laughter ]
-Save the wrapping.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.667
-Save the wrap
for the next year.

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.033
I go, "Yes, okay, Grandma."
-It's 3:00 p.m.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:38.501
-"All right, let's go!
Let's open up the thing," yeah.

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:40.167
It's so fun.
I look forward to it.

00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:42.901
Do you -- Are you a guy that
makes New Year's resolutions?

00:01:42.901 --> 00:01:44.400
-Eh...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.667
I check in on myself.
-You check in on yourself, yeah.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.934
-You know, I ask -- I ask --
I ask myself

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:52.467
to work on my patience
last year.

00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:55.467
-Yeah.
-On New Year's Eve.

00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:56.868
-We all need to do that.
-And I'm --

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:58.601
Now thank you for reminding me.
I guess tonight,

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:00.968
I'll start thinking about how
I've done the last 11 months.

00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:02.133
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly right.

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.601
Well, I think the last time
we talked --

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:06.133
I mean, congratulations.
Your book, "Greenlights"...

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:07.667
-Yeah.
-...was on "The New York Times"

00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:09.400
best-seller list.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.000
50, 5-0.
-Yeah.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.467
-50 weeks, it sold like
2.5 million copies.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.167
It is a giant, giant hit, bud.
Congratulations.

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:18.367
-Thank you.
-I loved it.

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:20.067
-Thank you.
-Did you -- Did you realize

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:21.701
it would take off like that?
I mean, so many people --

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:23.868
-I had no idea.
-It's massive.

00:02:23.868 --> 00:02:25.934
-I -- It -- it hit a nerve.
-Yeah.

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:27.634
-Thank you, everyone out there
who had already read it.

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:28.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:31.934
-It was so honest,
and it was so --

00:02:31.934 --> 00:02:34.000
I loved it.
I told you as soon as I read it,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.033
I couldn't stop reading it.
I go, "Oh, this is so --

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:39.367
it's so personal,
but it's so, also, universal."

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:41.234
-Yeah. I think that's what
people got from it.

00:02:41.234 --> 00:02:42.934
People said,
"Hey, I see myself in you.

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:45.567
I see my stories
in your stories."

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:48.601
It kind of lays out a map,
a process.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.434
It's a process book.
It is not a product book.

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:52.367
It's an approach to life

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.267
with this rodeo we're all
trying to get through

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:55.634
and trying to get
our 8 seconds on.

00:02:55.634 --> 00:02:57.100
-Yeah. "Greenlights" --
-How can we --

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.567
how can we do our best
to get it?

00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:00.801
-You -- Actually, you're --
you're coming out with a --

00:03:00.801 --> 00:03:02.534
a journal now,
a companion journal,

00:03:02.534 --> 00:03:05.033
to help people because I --

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:06.901
I think you were saying
in the book, too.

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:09.801
It's tough when you write --
to just tell someone,

00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:12.534
"Hey, just write something down
every day or every night."

00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:13.834
-A blank sheet of paper

00:03:13.834 --> 00:03:15.434
intimidates the heck out of us,
right?

00:03:15.434 --> 00:03:16.667
-It does.
-But journaling

00:03:16.667 --> 00:03:18.434
or writing things down,
I've done it all my life,

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:21.767
and it's how I wrote
"Greenlights," the book.

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:22.868
Immensely valuable.

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:25.200
I mean,
I write things down to forget.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.834
If you write them down,
you can forget them

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:28.200
'cause they're in there.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.300
Don't just go to the diary
or the journal to write down

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.667
when your frustrated
and confused.

00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:34.434
Go to the journal to write down
what you're doing in life

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:36.200
when you're rolling,
when you're catching

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.634
green lights,
when you're in the flow,

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:40.434
because we will
get out of flow again.

00:03:40.434 --> 00:03:42.434
And it's nice to have a document
to back and go, "Whew!

00:03:42.434 --> 00:03:44.667
What was I doing when I was --
when I was cruising?"

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.334
-Yeah, when life was perfect.

00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:49.467
-You know, you see habits and
you can change and recalibrate.

00:03:49.467 --> 00:03:51.400
But, yeah, it's a journal.
I got little nudges

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.100
along the way to kind of help
you and ask you some questions,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.234
to write your story.
-Yeah.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.534
-The book of "Greenlights"
was my story.

00:03:56.534 --> 00:03:58.067
This is for your story.
-Yeah, I love that, buddy.

00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:00.968
That's awesome.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:03.334
Congrats on that.
-Thank you.

00:04:03.334 --> 00:04:05.434
-We also -- You were nice enough
to Zoom into us

00:04:05.434 --> 00:04:07.868
when we couldn't
get any guests anywhere.

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:09.367
No one was allowed
to travel or do anything.

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:12.300
But there was talk of you maybe
running for governor of Texas.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.334
-Right.
-And you recently decided

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:17.200
to not run for governor.
-Yes.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.801
-Can you walk us
through that decision?

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:20.868
-Whew!
Well, it was a two-year

00:04:20.868 --> 00:04:23.968
consideration that I came
to the decision, really,

00:04:23.968 --> 00:04:26.167
over the last couple of months.
And I was asking myself

00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:27.801
the original question
and trying to answer,

00:04:27.801 --> 00:04:31.701
"How and where and what can I do
to be most useful to myself,

00:04:31.701 --> 00:04:33.968
to my family, and to
the most amount of people?"

00:04:33.968 --> 00:04:37.133
The embassy, the category
of politics came up,

00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:39.901
and it's a privileged one that
I gave great consideration to.

00:04:39.901 --> 00:04:41.667
But at this point in my life,
with the things --

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:44.234
I've got a 13-year-old,
an 11-year-old, an 8-year-old --

00:04:44.234 --> 00:04:45.334
the life I'm living right now,

00:04:45.334 --> 00:04:47.400
the storytelling
I want to keep doing,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.000
it's not the category for me
at this point in my life.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.334
-Still not ruling out future?
-I'm not until I am.

00:04:55.334 --> 00:04:56.701
-Okay.
-Someone told me that was

00:04:56.701 --> 00:04:57.968
a very McConaughey answer
the other day.

00:04:57.968 --> 00:04:59.868
[ Laughter ]
-"I'm not until I am."

00:04:59.868 --> 00:05:01.934
It's very -- yeah.
Yeah, that is.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:03.634
-I'll be keeping
an eye open for --

00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:05.367
-Because I know --
you know, Texas loves you.

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:09.501
And when we -- when we did
the show from Texas,

00:05:09.501 --> 00:05:10.968
you were nice enough
to come on the show.

00:05:10.968 --> 00:05:12.801
And, gosh, you're beloved
down there, as well.

00:05:12.801 --> 00:05:14.434
You think people love you here,
standing ovations,

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:16.634
they love you there.
-It's a great state.

00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:18.801
-I know you put on a benefit
concert with your wife...

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:20.634
-Yeah.
-...called "We're Texas."

00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:23.734
-Yeah.
-After that rough,

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:26.601
awful winter storm.
-Winter storm Uri came through.

00:05:26.601 --> 00:05:30.868
We're not used to winter storms
like that in Texas, okay?

00:05:30.868 --> 00:05:32.701
-No.
-We -- We were a bit awkward.

00:05:32.701 --> 00:05:34.601
We weren't prepared, and a lot
of people were out of home.

00:05:34.601 --> 00:05:35.901
A lot of people
were out of school.

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:37.234
A lot of people needed some help
because they didn't --

00:05:37.234 --> 00:05:38.767
they didn't
know how to deal with it.

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:42.167
And so, my foundation, which is
the just keep livin Foundation,

00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:44.234
we got together and said,
"We're not disaster relief,

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:46.834
but want to be disaster relief?"

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:49.934
We said, "Let's go."
And I headed to Texas.

00:05:49.934 --> 00:05:52.000
I got on the phone.
Camila got on the phone.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.200
100% of the people we called,
whether it was companies

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.267
who donated, whether it was
musicians who came in,

00:05:56.267 --> 00:05:58.934
everybody was a "yes."
And the first 30 seconds,

00:05:58.934 --> 00:06:01.234
we went down to Texas,
put the show on,

00:06:01.234 --> 00:06:05.534
and we raised over $7.7 million.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:05.534 --> 00:06:07.100
-That's how you do it
right there.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.467
You just stepped up, and I go,
"Oh, my gosh.

00:06:09.467 --> 00:06:11.400
What do you know about
putting on a live concert?"

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.934
You're like, "Nothing."
-It was, like, two takes today.

00:06:13.934 --> 00:06:16.601
You get -- No, you get one take.
You know, two maximum,

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:18.100
but let's throw it together
because you have to --

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.434
disaster relief,
you have to move quickly,

00:06:20.434 --> 00:06:22.334
because if you take your time
to say,

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:23.734
"Oh, this has
got to be perfect,"

00:06:23.734 --> 00:06:25.467
something else is taking
the front-page news.

00:06:25.467 --> 00:06:27.868
There's another disaster coming.
You know?

00:06:27.868 --> 00:06:29.501
So you need --
you need to jump on it quickly

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:31.767
and get everyone together
and take advantage

00:06:31.767 --> 00:06:33.901
of trying to get people to give
at that time

00:06:33.901 --> 00:06:36.667
when it is front-page news
and they know people need help.

00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:38.267
-I saw "Hollywood Reporter"
gave you --

00:06:38.267 --> 00:06:40.467
named you
Philanthropist of the Year

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:42.000
for all the stuff
you do to give back.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.567
-That was cool.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:43.567 --> 00:06:45.033
-It's good.
It's good to be,

00:06:45.033 --> 00:06:46.501
but it's also great
to give back.

00:06:46.501 --> 00:06:49.133
And it's also -- it's great.
You know what you do?

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:51.567
You -- You show up.
Every time, you always show up,

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:53.434
for me, but I think
for anything.

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:56.234
I know you're a great dad.
-Well, thank you.

00:06:56.234 --> 00:06:57.767
Try to.
I think we all try to.

00:06:57.767 --> 00:07:00.968
You know, I think we -- we learn
more and more as we age.

00:07:00.968 --> 00:07:03.033
We serve ourself
when we do serve others.

00:07:03.033 --> 00:07:05.968
That is a reciproc--
little exchange right there.

00:07:05.968 --> 00:07:07.767
-Yeah.
-And then, also, there's ways

00:07:07.767 --> 00:07:09.901
to serve ourself and serve
others at the same time.

00:07:09.901 --> 00:07:11.567
And where those two meet
when we're filling

00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:14.000
our bank account and our souls
account at the same time,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.801
we're getting the quantity and
the quality at the same time...

00:07:16.801 --> 00:07:18.200
-Yeah.
-Honeyhole.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.234
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's what I'm talking about.

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:22.968
I want to talk about
and show a clip from "Sing 2."

00:07:22.968 --> 00:07:24.634
More with Matthew McConaughey
when we come back, everybody.

00:07:24.634 --> 00:07:25.801
Come on back.
[ Cheers and applause ]

